1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:03,474
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Two.

2
00:00:03,474 --> 00:00:07,682
Construct at a given point a segment equal to a given segment.

3
00:00:07,682 --> 00:00:13,450
Given point A and segment B C, we are to construct at point A a segment equal to B C.

4
00:00:13,870 --> 00:00:17,043
From point A to point B, construct segment A B.

5
00:00:17,043 --> 00:00:20,892
On segment A B, construct the equilateral triangle D A B.

6
00:00:20,892 --> 00:00:24,807
Next, extend segments D A and D B continuously to E and F.

7
00:00:24,807 --> 00:00:30,681
Construct circle B C, with center B and defining radius point C, intersecting D F at G.

8
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:36,960
Next, construct circle D G, with center D and defining radius point G, intersecting D E at L.

9
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:41,938
Since B is the center of circle B C, segment B C equals segment B G.

10
00:00:41,938 --> 00:00:46,965
Again, since D is the center of circle D G, segment D L equals segment D G.

11
00:00:46,965 --> 00:00:56,214
Within these, D A equals D B from our equilateral triangle, so subtracting equals from equals, the remainder A L equals the remainder B G.

12
00:00:56,214 --> 00:01:02,380
Because both A L and B C equal B G, things equal to the same thing are equal to one another.

13
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:09,820
Therefore segment A L equals segment B C, and has been constructed at the given point A, as was required to do.
